<section class="page-head">
  <h1>Valuing new-to-world investments</h1>
  <p class="section-note">Building a shared framework for deciding what's worth investing in — when the thing has never existed.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study">
  <p class="masked-note">
    This case study has been deliberately masked. Specific metrics, thresholds, figures,
    names, and organizational details have been removed to protect confidential information.
    What remains is the shape of a problem I worked on and how I thought about it.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    An organization making new-to-world product bets kept hitting the same friction: there was
    no shared, written definition of what made a new investment "worth it." Proposals bounced —
    reworked again and again against a bar nobody had actually articulated. Underneath the
    process pain sat a genuinely hard question: <strong>how do you value something that has
    never existed?</strong> There are no comparables, adoption is a guess, and every figure in
    the model is an assumption stacked on another assumption.
  </p>

  <h2>How I framed it</h2>
  <p>
    I treated it as a trade-off, not a formula — return against risk, and the near term against
    the long term. A single return metric (an IRR, say) is precise math on invented inputs; on
    its own it flatters short-term thinking and punishes the slow-then-steep curve most new
    categories actually follow. So the question I kept asking wasn't "what's the number?" but
    "is the upside big enough, and are we willing to fund the uncertainty long enough to find
    out?"
  </p>

  <h2>What I did</h2>
  <p>
    I evaluated several candidate approaches, weighed what each one captured and what it quietly
    ignored, and recommended a small, combined view rather than any single "golden arrow." Two
    design constraints mattered as much as the math: it had to be <strong>simple enough for
    non-finance partners to actually use</strong>, and <strong>honest enough to say what it
    couldn't tell you</strong>. A framework only creates value if people adopt it and trust it.
    The real deliverable wasn't a spreadsheet — it was a shared language and an upfront
    definition of "good," so a hard call could be made faster and with less subjectivity.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest caveat</h2>
  <p>
    This isn't the "right" answer. It's one approach, at one point in time, meant to be argued
    with and revised. What I keep coming back to is building the mental models that let a team
    reason together about something genuinely uncertain — and then holding them loosely enough
    to change when the evidence does.
  </p>

